Q: Can C++ have code in the global scope? A: <p>As a complement to <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/51886355/21691539">Nicol Bolas answer</a>, the grammar of the language, as described in the standard, allows for declarations at namescape scope but not for expressions (such as an assignment), which require, for example a function block scope.</p>
<p>I guess that the rational behind it is that it would be excessively difficult to standardize the behavior (when should an expression be executed ? how many time if in a shared header ?) for very limited benefits. Read about static initialization order fiasco to have an idea about this kind of issue.</p>
<p>NB: it can be worked around by using an initialization whose right hand side is a function call, the desired expressions being executed inside this function scope:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>int i = 0;  // declaration/initialization
/*
i = 1;  // not allowed at global namespace scope
int j = i;
*/
// expression nested inside a (lambda-)function call: allowed
int j = [](int&amp; x) {
    x = 1;
    return x;
}(i);
int main() { return i+j; }
</code></pre>
